    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d155">

                <pb facs="0269" n="181" sameAs="frus:frus1904/pg181"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Conger to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1904-10-23"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">October 23,
                    1904</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>(Mr. Conger reports the proposal of the Chinese Government concerning the payment
                    of the indemnity.</p>
                <list>
                    <item>First. That the rate of exchange hereafter shall be fixed monthly upon the
                        average daily rate, instead of taking the rate of the day of payment.</item>
                    <item>Second. That interest on the arrears resulting from payments at silver
                        instead of gold rates up to January 1 next shall be canceled.</item>
                    <item>Third. That interest at 4 per cent per annum shall be allowed on all
                        interest and amortization deposited monthly in designated banks before
                        due.</item>
                </list>
                <p>The Chinese Government promises to pay, in January next, the accrued difference
                    between silver and gold payments, and all succeeding payments when due at a gold
                    rate, if the above proposal is accepted by the powers. Mr. Conger requests to be
                    instructed what course he is to take.)</p>
            </div>
        </body>
